USE OF THE ABAP DEVELOPMENT TOOLS (ADT) PROTOCOL
------------------------------------------------
This server talks to SAP through the REST protocol of ABAP Development Tools
(/sap/bc/adt/...), the same one Eclipse ADT uses, via the open-source library
abap-adt-api. Please read the following before using it with an SAP system.

1. It is not a released SAP API. The ADT protocol is not published in the SAP
   Business Accelerator Hub and carries no stability contract: SAP may change or
   remove endpoints between releases, and SAP does not support third-party ADT
   clients. To limit that risk, the server reads each system's ADT discovery,
   reports when a function is not available in that release instead of assuming
   it, and fails with an explicit error rather than an empty or partial result.

2. It is used like an IDE, not as an integration. Each person connects with their
   own named SAP user, with their own developer license and authorizations
   (e.g. S_DEVELOP), exactly as with Eclipse ADT. The server never extends those
   permissions; it can only restrict them (read-only by system, no writes outside
   development systems, human confirmation and an audit log for every change).

3. Do not run it with a shared technical user. Serving several people, or an
   automated process, through one technical SAP user changes the nature of the
   access (it may count as indirect/digital access under your SAP agreement) and
   breaks per-person authorization and traceability. This project is designed
   and documented for named users only.

4. Review your SAP agreement. Licensing terms differ per customer. Before rolling
   it out, check with whoever manages your SAP contract how your agreement treats
   developer tools and indirect access. Nothing in this notice is legal advice.

